TI  - Protective Effects of Paeoniflorin Against Inflammation and Oxidative Stress in Rats with Gestational Diabetes Mellitus via Inhibiting the RhoA/ROCK Signaling Pathway
PY  - 2024
T2  - Revista Brasileira de Farmacognosia
VL  - 34
IS  - 3
SP  - 536
EP  - 552
DO  - 10.1007/s43450-023-00505-4
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85181200604&doi=10.1007%2fs43450-023-00505-4&partnerID=40&md5=3d83f45b4e6a8e5c6896483b696ee470
AB  - Gestational diabetes mellitus is a condition of glucose intolerance that occurs only in pregnant women. Paeoniflorin was reported to possess anti-inflammation and antioxidant properties, and it has a potential to alleviate gestational diabetes mellitus symptoms. This study aims to confirm whether paeoniflorin attenuates placental inflammation and oxidative stress in gestational diabetes mellitus and investigate the underlying mechanism. Streptozotocin-provoked gestational diabetes mellitus rat model was utilized for in vivo assays. High glucose-stimulated human trophoblast cell line HTR-8/SVneo was used to mimic in vitro gestational diabetes mellitus environment. Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay was conducted to detect insulin concentration and plasma leptin level of rats, the levels of inflammatory cytokines and oxidative stress markers in both rats and HTR-8/SVneo cells. RhoA, ROCK1, and ROCK2 protein levels were tested via western blot analysis. Placenta tissues of gestational diabetes mellitus patients and healthy pregnant women were subjected to quantitative real-time polymerase chain reaction and western blot analyses to evaluate RhoA and ROCK1 expression. Furthermore, paeoniflorin-treated gestational diabetes mellitus rats and HTR-8/SVneo cells were treated with lysophosphatidic acid, the RhoA activator, to validate the role of RhoA/ROCK pathway in paeoniflorin-mediated inflammation and oxidative stress in vitro and in vivo. Paeoniflorin administration mitigated the dysregulation of blood glucose, insulin concentration, plasma leptin, and body weight in maternal rats with gestational diabetes mellitus. Placental inflammation and oxidative stress in gestational diabetes mellitus rats as well as high glucose-induced inflammation and oxidative stress in HTR-8/SVneo cells were alleviated by paeoniflorin treatment. Additionally, RhoA and ROCK mRNA and protein levels were significantly higher in gestational diabetes mellitus patients than in healthy pregnant women. The levels of proteins in the RhoA/ROCK pathway were substantially reduced by paeoniflorin treatment in gestational diabetes mellitus rats and high glucose-stimulated HTR-8/SVneo cells. Nevertheless, lysophosphatidic acid treatment reversed the anti-inflammatory and antioxidant effects of paeoniflorin in vitro and in vivo. Paeoniflorin effectively hindered gestational diabetes mellitus progression by ameliorating inflammation and oxidative stress via inactivating the RhoA/ROCK pathway. Graphical Abstract: (Figure presented.) © The Author(s) under exclusive licence to Sociedade Brasileira de Farmacognosia 2023.
