VL  - 30
IS  - 5
SP  - 717
EP  - 721
DO  - 10.1016/j.cardfail.2023.12.008
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85184046673&doi=10.1016%2fj.cardfail.2023.12.008&partnerID=40&md5=20fe3da14c700c0a1de6a17309a6b25d
AB  - Peripartum cardiomyopathy (PPCM) is a rare but significant cause of new-onset heart failure (HF) during the peri- and post-partum periods. Advances in GDMT for HF with reduced ventricular function have led to substantial improvements in survival and quality of life, yet few studies examine the longitudinal care received by patients with PPCM. The aim of this research is to address this gap by retrospectively characterizing patients with PPCM across a multihospital health system and investigating the frequency of cardiology and HF specialty referrals. Understanding whether surveillance and medical management differ among patients referred to HF will help to underscore the importance of referring patients with PPCM to HF specialists for optimal care. © 2023 Elsevier Inc.
