TI  - Maternal and Fetal Conditions With Consequences for the Fetal Brain
PY  - 2024
T2  - Volpe’s Neurology of the Newborn, Seventh Edition
SP  - 263.e1
EP  - 289.e15
DO  - 10.1016/B978-0-443-10513-5.00011-5
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85198490708&doi=10.1016%2fB978-0-443-10513-5.00011-5&partnerID=40&md5=47010db31c0b0e1df7b7f413a59a104d
AB  - This chapter examines two maternal conditions that affect fetal brain development—disorders of the maternal endocrine system and maternal mental health disorders—and also offers congenital heart disease as a model of how fetal conditions affect fetal brain development. Regarding maternal endocrine disturbance, our understanding is more advanced for insulin, thyroid hormone, and glucocorticoids; these will be the primary focus of this section. Maternal mental health disorders of pregnancy are now recognized as the most common complication of pregnancy; their incidence, causation, and management, as well as the potential pathways and manifestations of neurological sequelae in the fetal brain and infant, will be discussed. In the final section, we examine the role of prenatal factors leading to neurological morbidity in congenital heart disease and to differences in brain maturation between those fetuses and gestational age–matched controls; we also discuss strategies likely to reduce systemic complications, improve brain-oriented care, and reduce the risks they pose to the developing brain. In summary, the developing brain of the fetus and newborn may experience multiple types of insults that cause disrupted development and destructive injury, with potential lifelong consequences. © 2025 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
