TI  - Pregnancy-associated risk factors for future cardiovascular disease–early prevention strategies warranted
PY  - 2024
T2  - Climacteric
VL  - 27
IS  - 1
SP  - 41
EP  - 46
DO  - 10.1080/13697137.2023.2287628
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85181504185&doi=10.1080%2f13697137.2023.2287628&partnerID=40&md5=126c570a142d108da405c4018c6fffb7
AB  - We summarize convincing evidence that future cardiovascular disease (CVD) risk increases one-fold to four-fold for women with a history of pregnancy complicated by hypertensive disorders, gestational diabetes, fetal growth restriction, placental abruption and preterm birth. A concomitant occurrence of two or more complications in the same pregnancy further potentiates the risk. These women should be informed of their future CVD risks during the postpartum check-up taking place after delivery, and also, if needed, treated, for example, for persisting high blood pressure. In these women with high blood pressure, check-up should take place within 7–10 days, and if severe hypertension, within 72 h. Women without diagnostic signs and symptoms should be examined for the first time 1–2 years postpartum and then at intervals of 2–3 years for a complete CVD risk profile including clinical and laboratory assessments. Women should be informed for future CVD risks and their effective prevention with healthy lifestyle factors. Combined oral contraceptives should be avoided or used with caution. If laboratory or other clinical findings indicate, then vigorous treatments consisting of non-medical and medical (antihypertensives, statins, antidiabetic and anti-obesity therapies) interventions should be initiated early with liberal indications and with ambitious therapeutic goals. Low-dose aspirin and menopausal hormone therapy should be used in selected cases. Active control and treatment policies of these women with pregnancy-related risks will likely result in decreases of CVD occurrence in later life. © 2024 International Menopause Society.
