TI  - Severe pre-eclampsia as a rare cause of profound hyponatraemia
PY  - 2023
T2  - BMJ Case Reports
VL  - 16
IS  - 9
C7  - e253881
DO  - 10.1136/bcr-2022-253881
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85169518027&doi=10.1136%2fbcr-2022-253881&partnerID=40&md5=228b8d2da11190954fa37e6bfc5927cf
AB  - Profound hyponatraemia, defined as sodium <125 mmol/L, is a very rare complication of pre-eclampsia (PET) with a relative paucity of cases reported. Pre-eclampsia is a multisystem disorder with a maternal mortality of up to 20%. Hyponatraemia is associated with disease severity, twin pregnancy, advanced maternal age, in vitro fertilisation and HELLP (haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes and low platelets). The authors present the case of a low-risk nulliparous woman presenting with frontal headache and normal BP at 31 +2 weeks gestation. Laboratory investigations confirmed a sodium of 123 mmol/L. Her urine protein creatinine ratio was 322 mg/mmol. She developed PET (BP 171/100 mm Hg) refractory to pharmacological management. She underwent an emergency lower segment caesarean section and was delivered of a live neonate. The maternal serum sodium normalised within 24 hours. Hyponatraemia should be regarded as a marker of severity in the setting of pre-eclampsia and may be an indication for an expedited delivery. Prompt management is required to prevent convulsions, maternal mortality and adverse fetal outcomes.  © BMJ Publishing Group Limited 2023. No commercial re-use. See rights and permissions. Published by BMJ.
