TI  - Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS): Clinical features, risk factors, biomarkers, treatment, and therapeutic strategies
PY  - 2023
T2  - Toxicology and Human Health: Environmental Exposures and Biomarkers
SP  - 197
EP  - 229
DO  - 10.1007/978-981-99-2193-5_9
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85171502626&doi=10.1007%2f978-981-99-2193-5_9&partnerID=40&md5=9df4b74f758a85fb63c8309188664bf9
AB  - Among reproductive-age women, the most predominant reproductive-metabolic endocrinopathy is polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS). It is of unknown etiology. Women with PCOS experience menstrual dysfunction, androgen overload, obesity, insulin resistance, diabetes and gestational diabetes mellitus, inflammation, endothelial dysfunctioning, cardiovascular disease (CVD), miscarriage, cancer, pre-eclampsia, and psychological problems all of which harm their quality of life. Many biomarkers of inflammation, endothelial dysfunctioning, reproductive and endocrine disorders, diabetes mellitus, and cardiovascular diseases were reported to be elevated in PCOS. Most notably inflammatory biomarkers like adipokines, chemokines, tumor necrosis factor (TNF-α), C-reactive protein (CRP), and several microRNAs (miRNAs) were reported to be related to the metabolic and reproductive disorders of PCOS. Many treatment strategies notably oral contraceptive pills (OCPs), metformin, thiazolidinediones, spironolactone, aromatase inhibitors, gonadotropins, laparoscopic ovarian drilling (LOD), and in vitro fertilization (IVF) are available for the treatment of PCOS. Strategies based on evidences are required to direct physicians in the effective management of PCOS. Current diagnostic criteria are still ambiguous, and patients with less serious non-classic phenotypes can go undetected. There is a need for developing more specific and effective strategies in its early diagnosis and prevention of associated morbidities. This chapter summarizes the several biomarkers and treatment aspects that can be taken into consideration currently in its management. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. All rights reserved.
