TI  - Consideration of the Pathophysiology and Treatment of Type 1 and Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus and Other Diabetes Mellitus Subtypes
PY  - 2023
T2  - Current Trends in the Diagnosis and Management of Metabolic Disorders
SP  - 235
EP  - 253
DO  - 10.1201/9781003384823-14
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85180048756&doi=10.1201%2f9781003384823-14&partnerID=40&md5=c9bbd1554854a909f974838c78113ece
AB  - Diabetes mellitus is a complex disease process affecting a large portion of the United States adult population. In 2022, 11.3% of people aged 18 years or older in the United States had diabetes mellitus, while 38% met the criteria for pre-diabetes. These criteria include either two abnormal blood glucose tests in the asymptomatic individual (glucose >100 mg/dL) or one abnormal blood glucose test in the symptomatic individual (>100 mg/dL). These tests are typically performed on the fasting patient who has not consumed any food products in 8 hours or more. These criteria elucidate the pathophysiology of the disease itself. An individual with diabetes mellitus, either type 1 or type 2, has a relative insulin deficiency and therefore cannot regulate glucose concentrations in the bloodstream. This is reflected in the clinical manifestations of elevated blood glucose levels, increased appetite, weight loss, and increased urination. The diagnosis of diabetes mellitus significantly increases the risk of developing other health conditions, including cardiovascular disease, which can lead to myocardial infarction, peripheral arterial disease, heart failure, and damage to other organs. The need for tight regulation of blood glucose concentrations is imperative but often difficult due to the cost, storage, side effects, and administration of the necessary medications. Although diabetes mellitus type 1 and type 2 are the most well-known subtypes of the overarching term “diabetes”, there are many variations of diabetes that fall under this umbrella that are equally as complex and important. These include mature-onset diabetes of the young (MODY), gestational diabetes, and diabetes insipidus, which in fact has no effect on blood glucose. The scientific community is learning more and more about the genetic as well as environmental components of these disease processes and improving diagnostic tools yearly. Through continued research, the hope is to create more individualized treatment plans for optimal regulation and, eventually, prevention of these diseases altogether. © 2024 selection and editorial matter, Seth K. Amponsah, Emmanuel K. Ofori, Yashwant V. Pathak; individual chapters, the contributors.
