AB  - Diabetes is a chronic disease that develops as a result of the lack or inability of endogenous insulin to function. The prevalence of diabetes has been increasing for the last 50 years as a result of our increasingly industrialized diet and decreased physical activity. Diabetes is one of the important causes of morbidity and mortality in today's people with increased life expectancy. It is critical to assess people for risk factors, provide nutrition and exercise recommendations, diagnose and treat diabetes early, and recognize and prevent complications early in order to protect them from diabetes and complications. Diabetes mellitus is classified as Type I diabetes, Type II diabetes, Gestational diabetes and other specific types of diabetes. Nutrition and exercise recommendations are the first step in prevention and treatment. Insulin injections and some oral antidiabetic agents are used in medical treatment. Acute complications of diabetes are diabetic ketoacidosis, hyperglycemic hyperosmolar condition, lactic acidosis and hyperglycemia, and these conditions can be mortal. Chronic complications are macrovascular events such as coronary artery disease, peripheral artery disease, and stroke and microvascular events such as retinopathy, nephropathy, and neuropathy that are mostly related to morbidity and cause long-term mortality. © 2022 Nova Science Publishers, Inc.
