TY  - CHAP
AU  - Anagnostis, P.
AU  - Goulis, D.G.
TI  - Introduction
PY  - 2022
T2  - Comprehensive Clinical Approach to Diabetes During Pregnancy
SP  - 1
EP  - 9
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-030-89243-2_1
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85153640659&doi=10.1007%2f978-3-030-89243-2_1&partnerID=40&md5=c0c4004ef8699f2bf294e95085dbf3e7
AB  - Diabetes mellitus (DM) during pregnancy constitutes a common disorder of glucose homeostasis, with several short- and long-term maternal and offspring adverse outcomes. It can manifest either as pre-existing or as DM initially diagnosed during pregnancy (gestational DM, GDM). This classification is crucial, since these two entities differ in terms of their impact on maternal and neonatal health, with GDM being the most prevalent (85% of cases). Concerning pathophysiology, GDM simulates type 2 DM (T2DM), as a corollary of Β-cell dysfunction on a background of chronic insulin resistance. The prevalence of GDM differs according to the definition (3.8-7.8% in Europe). Several risk factors contribute to the development of DM during pregnancy, such as obesity, excessive gestational weight gain, polycystic ovary syndrome, ethnicity, genetic polymorphisms, advanced maternal age, as well as family and individual history of GDM. Maternal complications include increased risk of preterm birth, arterial hypertension, preeclampsia, cesarian delivery, and increased risk of T2DM and cardiovascular disease in later life. Short-term fetal risks include macrosomia, shoulder dystocia, birth trauma, hyperbilirubinemia, and postpartum hypoglycemia. Of note, GDM may “program” the exposed offspring to a higher risk of cardiometabolic complications in their adult life. A multidisciplinary approach is proposed, including medical nutrition therapy for optimal weight gain and close glucose monitoring, as well as pre-pregnancy healthcare programs, to avert the development of GDM. Several issues, such as diagnostic thresholds during early pregnancy, optimal monitoring after delivery, and efficacy of pharmaceutical intervention during the pre-conception period, need to be resolved in future studies. This book provides a thorough approach to the diagnosis, epidemiology, pathophysiology, maternal and neonatal outcomes, management, and future perspectives of this complex clinical entity. © The Editor(s) (if applicable) and The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2022.
