AB  - Objective: To summarize the published literature on the economic burden of polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) Design: A review of the published literature on the economic burden of PCOS. Discussion of economic burden analyses and their importance in healthcare research funding. Results: To date, only two studies have attempted to estimate the economic burden of PCOS. Together, the estimated cost of initial diagnostic evaluation, treatment of common presenting symptoms during the reproductive years (menstrual irregularity/abnormal uterine bleeding, hirsutism, infertility), associated pregnancy complications (gestational diabetes, gestational hypertension, preeclampsia), and long-term health consequences (type 2 diabetes and stroke), the total estimated cost of PCOS annually in 2020 USD is $7.9 billion. Conclusion: Given the prevalence and economic impact of this disease, improved research funding and education of clinicians, patients, policymakers, and the general public is warranted. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2022. All rights reserved.
