SP  - 133
EP  - 146
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-030-89243-2_8
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85153638913&doi=10.1007%2f978-3-030-89243-2_8&partnerID=40&md5=c2f3625b3162e339a70e137779542f10
AB  - In most healthcare systems, women with diabetes in pregnancy are offered more intensive antenatal care, as this condition has adverse pregnancy outcomes. In particular, an association of preexisting diabetes with increased congenital defects and stillbirth rates has been identified. In contrast, poor glycemic control, even in gestational diabetes mellitus, has been correlated with increased risk for macrosomia and shoulder dystocia during delivery. Therefore, intensive obstetrical monitoring, including a multidisciplinary approach and more scheduled scans, are considered appropriate elements of diabetic pregnant women’s care. At a minimum, the care for pregnancies complicated by diabetes would include an early viability scan, followed by a nuchal translucency and an anomaly scan, along with fetal echocardiography by an expert in cases with preexisting diabetes. Individualized fetal surveillance with a combination of cardiotocography, ultrasound, biophysical profile, and fetal movements assessment is recommended during the third trimester. Despite the great progress made in the management of diabetic pregnant women during the past decades, there is still limited evidence on the optimal frequency and method of fetal monitoring. © The Editor(s) (if applicable) and The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2022.
