UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85153632553&doi=10.1007%2f978-3-030-89243-2_15&partnerID=40&md5=a47c72dad08faf31f849c3d328fd097c
AB  - Gestational and pre-existing diabetes require specific management during parturition to prevent fetal acidemia and neonatal hypoglycemia. Moreover, regarding the timing of delivery, the increased risk of stillbirth and shoulder dystocia should be balanced against the risk of neonatal lung immaturity when delivered too early and the possibility of cesarean section, because of failed induction of labor. A cesarean section should be offered according to regular obstetric indications, except for cases of excessively macrosomic fetuses. Management of preterm labor is the same as for non-diabetic women, but the administration of betamimetics should be avoided. A multi-disciplinary approach by a maternal-fetal medicine specialist and an endocrinologist should be adopted during the intrapartum period. For cases with pre-existing diabetes, the delivery should take place in a tertiary-care center with continuous intrapartum fetal heart rate monitoring. Obstetric management should be accompanied by regular evaluation of maternal blood glucose levels to prevent adverse neonatal outcomes. During the puerperium, breastfeeding should be encouraged, and appropriate family planning and long-term screening should be offered to all women. © The Editor(s) (if applicable) and The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2022.
