TI  - Stem cells: An exciting avenue for diabetes mellitus
PY  - 2021
T2  - Stem Cells in Disease Pathogenesis
SP  - 275
EP  - 296
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85114959699&partnerID=40&md5=cd92bf08fc6620dc79c680a24ae37681
AB  - Diabetes is a group of chronic metabolic diseases in which blood sugar levels are increased. There are different types of diabetes i.e., type 1 diabetes, type 2 diabetes, gestational diabetes, and other specific type monogenic diabetes). The prevalence of diabetes is increasing globally with more socioeconomic consequences; therefore, high blood sugar creates the symptoms of frequent urination, increased thirst, and increased hunger. Untreated, diabetes causes many complications; acute complications include diabetic ketoacidosis, serious complications include heart disease, stroke, kidney failure, foot ulcers, and retinopathy. In diabetic condition the pancreas do not produce enough amount of insulin, or the pancreatic cells do not respond properly to the insulin produced by the body. There are many pharmacological treatments available for the management of hyperglycemia, but recently there are advance treatments available like stem cells therapy, gene therapy, diabetes vaccine etc. Stem cell therapy is one of the emerging treatments for diabetes. Stem cells help in pancreatic regeneration, also increase the functioning of β-cells, angiogenesis, and immunomodulation and hence maintain normal blood sugar level in diabetics. © 2021 Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved.
