TI  - Diabetes in pregnancy-Gestational diabetes mellitus
PY  - 2021
T2  - Diabetes and Diabetic Complications: Current Status and Future Prospective
SP  - 289
EP  - 324
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85109467356&partnerID=40&md5=c6706c3ffc0da29c60877714872051f3
AB  - Diabetes mellitus associated with intolerance of carbohydrate may occur in pregnancy due to insulin resistance and the environment of diabetogenic stress leading to the compensatory increase in the insulin secretion by the action of the placental hormones. The hyperglycemic episodes which are detected first during pregnancy lead to diagnosis of Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM). The risk factors for GDM include excessive gestational weight gain, obesity, advanced maternal age, family and personal history of GDM, intrauterine environment (low or high birth weight), and other diseases of insulin resistance. The consequences of GDM comprise increased risk of hypertensive states, hyperinsulinemia and lipid abnormalities that may proceed to the incidence of cardiovascular diseases and occurrence of metabolic diseases. There is also an increased risk of congenital abnormalities, macrosomia, stillbirth, infection and the other complications in the foetus. Patient education about the effectual medical nutrition therapy and the self-monitoring of the blood glucose levels is crucial. A in-depth comprehensive study of the associated physiological factors, pathological mechanisms and the genetic associations would further help in the identification, diagnostic screening and thus effective management of the patient by virtue of pharmacological and lifestyle modification strategies to achieve euglycemia and thus put an end to this cycle of transmission. The aim of this article is to critically and thoroughly review GDM in an elaborative and comprehensive manner, its management during labour, postnatal period and pre-existing diabetic conditions along with effective follow-up practices along with providing an insight into the recent pharmacotherapeutic approaches. © 2021 Nova Science Publishers, Inc.
