TI  - Gestational Diabetes: Importance of Follow-up Screening for the Benefit of Long-term Health
PY  - 2020
T2  - Obstetrics and Gynecology Clinics of North America
VL  - 47
IS  - 3
SP  - 383
EP  - 396
DO  - 10.1016/j.ogc.2020.04.002
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85085616769&doi=10.1016%2fj.ogc.2020.04.002&partnerID=40&md5=46b946081ecd74e5954ad5a7b8920b12
AB  - Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) is the most common obstetric metabolic disorder. Long-term health consequences, including type 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome, and cardiovascular disease, are common with GDM. Postpartum glucose screening is recommended for women with a prior GDM pregnancy. Rates of postpartum screening compliance remain low. Interventions ranging from appointment reminder systems to personalized chronic disease education are being used, emphasizing future chronic disease risk for patients with a history of GDM. With these practice changes, clinicians can more thoroughly engage in the early identification, intervention, and prevention of chronic disease for women with a history of GDM. © 2020
