AB  - Objective: By comparing the changes of brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) between twin pregnancy and singleton pregnancy during pregnancy, delivery and postpartum period, to explored the status of heart load in twins pregnancy at different stages. Methods: A total of 252 patients admitted to the obstetics and Gynecology Hospital of Fudan University from Jul 2015 to Apr 2017 were incloded in our study and were analyzd retrospectively. The gestational week of delivery was set at 28-41 weeks of gestation. According to the same admission and exclusion criteria, pregnant women were divided into twins pregnancy group and single pregnancy group. The two groups were further divided into prenatal, day of delivery, the first day and second day after delivery according to the time of pregnancy and puerperium. Nonparametric test was used to analyze the differences between different groups. Results: A total of 252 pregnant women were enrolled. There was no significant difference in age between the two groups. Pregnancy with anemia (Hgb>80 g/L), the proportion of gestational diabetes mellitus between the two groups at different time points had no statistical difference. There was no significant difference in BNP level between the two groups before and on the day of delivery and on the secend day of delivery. The BNP level of twins pregnancy group was significantly higher than that of singleton pregnancy group on the first day of delivery (P<0. 05). According to the subgroup analysis of delivery mode, there was significant difference in BNP level between the two groups on the first day after cesarean section (P<0. 05). There was statistical difference in BNP distribution between vaginal delivery and cesarean section in singleton pregnancy group on the first day and the second day after delivery (P<0. 05). Conclusion: BNP was the highest on the first day after delivery in singleton and twins pregnancy group, and the heart load of twins pregnancy was significantly heavier than that of singleton pregnancy. © 2020, Editorial Department of Fudan University Journal of Medical Sciences. All right reserved.
