TI  - CONGENITAL MALFORMATIONS IN GESTATIONAL DIABETES MELLITUS
PY  - 2020
T2  - Gestational Diabetes: From Diagnosis to Treatment
SP  - 431
EP  - 438
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85148799747&partnerID=40&md5=7b7a479078f157b88e6a306f73d2a584
AB  - Congenital malformations play a significant role in infant and early childhood deaths and morbidity. Diabetes while pregnancy increases anomaly risk. In another words, Maternal diabetes is a well-established and increasingly prevalent environmental risk factor for gestational diabetes; however if exposure to high serum glucose levels earlier, especially during first trimester, it could cause congenital malformations, too. The most common type of congenital malformation that is seen due to gestational diabetes, is congenital heart disease. Cardiomyopathies are especially frequent. But neurologic, gastrointestinal and other anomalies are also reported in the literature. Because gestational diabetes is diagnosed after week 20, the risk of malformation is rare. Even if there is slightly increased risk at gestational diabetes, the hope of doctors is to manage screening of at-risk mothers closely and prevent congenital anomalies. © 2020 by Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved.
