AU  - Yung, H.W.
AU  - Burton, G.J.
TI  - PLACENTAL ENDOPLASMIC RETICULUM STRESS IN THE DETERMINATION OF PREGNANCY OUTCOME: FROM HOMEOSTASIS TO PATHOPHYSIOLOGY
PY  - 2020
T2  - Encyclopedia of Cell Biology: New Research (9 Volume Set)
VL  - 4
SP  - 811
EP  - 836
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85169345502&partnerID=40&md5=51ec2c2ed016ac9c9bb9e7c60c5910cc
AB  - Adverse consequences of pregnancy disorders not only threaten the lives of the unborn baby and its mother, but also pose an increased risk of chronic poor health, such as obesity, type II diabetes, and cardiovascular disease, for both individuals in later life. It is estimated that approximately one-fifth of human pregnancies suffer from various types of pregnancy disorders, and fetal growth restriction (FGR), pre-eclampsia (PE), and gestational diabetes account for the majority of cases. In the last decade, increasing evidence has demonstrated that endoplasmic reticulum (ER) stress plays a central role in the causation of many human pathologies, including metabolic syndromes, neurodegenerative diseases and cancers. Furthermore, the molecular mechanisms involved have been widely explored and documented. In contrast, research on ER stress in pregnancy disorders is in its infancy. Placental dysfunction is well recognized to be central to many of these disorders. The placenta is a transient organ that exists only at the time of the pregnancy, and is vital for its success. The placenta forms the interface between the mother and her fetus, functioning like a “life-support machine” as well as shielding the fetus from maternal immune attack. It also secretes large quantities of hormones and growth factors into the maternal circulation that modulate maternal physiology and metabolism in order to meet the demands of the growing fetus. We therefore speculate that the placenta, like the pancreas, is susceptible to ER stress. Our group was the first to report the existence of ER stress in placentas from pregnancies complicated by FGR and PE in 2008. Since then, we have identified that activation of ER stress pathways may also play a more physiological role in the regulation of placental development, for example in pregnancies at high altitude where it most likely represents a homeostatic adaptation to the chronic hypobaric hypoxia experienced. ER stress can act as a “double-edged sword, ” to ensure the survival of cells under stressed conditions, but also to promote cell death in order to eliminate irreversibly damaged cells. In this chapter, we provide evidence for the existence of ER stress in placentas from a number of pregnancy disorders, and outline the potential molecular mechanisms involved. We then discuss how the severity of placental ER stress may play a crucial role in determining pregnancy outcome. Furthermore, the potential use of ER stress biomarkers as early diagnostic tools in pregnancy disorders is discussed, and the molecular signature for classifying subtypes of PE evaluated. © 2020 Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved.
