AB  - Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM) is regarded as one of the most common complications of pregnancy. GDM has been related to dyslipidemia, T2DM, subsequent hypertension, atherosclerosis, and vascular dysfunction and other markers of cardiovascular risk. Therefore, both women with GDM and children are at high risk for various metabolic abnormalities such as cardiovascular disease (CVD). Although major CVD events, such as stroke or myocardial infarction, are positively related to GDM in many studies, the risk of CVD in women with GDM is not understood in depth. Despite there are reports showing lifestyle changes and some pharmacological interventions might reduce the risk, the management strategies during long-term follow-up remain unclear. This chapter is about the relationship between the history of GDM and CVD, and to discuss the prevention strategies. © 2020 by Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved.
