TI  - The place of fetal cardiac functional assessment in high risk obstetrics
PY  - 2017
T2  - Obstetrics and Gynaecology Forum
VL  - 27
IS  - 2
SP  - 11
EP  - 13
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85026465291&partnerID=40&md5=4ec949562ae4fc50e93055763b4c266b
AB  - The fetal heart is significantly impacted in a number of high risk obstetric conditions in particular gestational diabetes, intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR), and pre-eclampsia from different pathophysiological pathways. These include abnormal metabolic metabolism in gestational diabetes, hypoxia in growth restriction and increased afterload in pre-eclampsia. Cardiac function using the myocardial performance index (MPI) and E/A ratio in a series of studies performed at our unit, was shown to be altered in fetuses in poorly controlled gestational diabetes, intrauterine growth restriction and preeclampsia and links to adverse perinatal outcome have been suggested in these conditions. The MPI has a unique property in that it can easily be tracked and becomes abnormal before hypoxia or acidosis sets in and as such this parameter can be used as a monitoring tool for assessing deteriorating cardiovascular function thereby potentially guiding the physician to optimal timing of delivery before overt acidosis, myocardial necrosis or perinatal death sets in. The MPI and E/A ratio could potentially be integrated into routine fetal monitoring models.
