UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85041243453&doi=10.1016%2fB978-0-12-803412-5.00006-9&partnerID=40&md5=0914480af3f04d5a1ab95e7abc8e4a48
AB  - Diabetes is characterized by an elevated blood glucose and is classified into subtypes including type 1 diabetes, type 2 diabetes, gestational diabetes, and diabetes secondary to other medical conditions. Diabetes is a risk factor for several other conditions, including cardiovascular (CV) disease, nephropathy, neuropathy, and ocular disease. The clinical chemistry laboratory plays an essential role not only in the assessment of glycemia for diagnosing and monitoring diabetes but also in biochemical risk assessments for CV disease. Testing for these conditions makes up a very large volume of work for clinical chemistry laboratories and relies on colorimetric assays, enzymatic assays, turbidimetric/nephelometric assays, immunoassays, chromatography, and mass spectrometry. It is essential that clinicians understand the strengths and limitations of these tests, as well as specimen collection protocols and how to correctly interpret results to optimally manage patients with diabetes and its associated conditions. © 2017 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
