TI  - Dysregulation of 11beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenases: implications during pregnancy and beyond
PY  - 2017
T2  - Journal of Maternal-Fetal and Neonatal Medicine
VL  - 30
IS  - 3
SP  - 284
EP  - 293
DO  - 10.3109/14767058.2016.1171308
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84964354799&doi=10.3109%2f14767058.2016.1171308&partnerID=40&md5=6be972f93a39ac5d287b741df98185fb
AB  - Glucococorticoids play a critical role in the developmental programing and fetal growth. Key molecules mediating and regulating tissue-specific glucocorticoid actions are 11beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase (11beta-HSD) type 1 and 2 isozymes, both of which are expressed in the placenta and the fetal membranes. 11beta-HSD1 is implicated in the pathogenesis of metabolic syndrome and its dysregulation has been observed in pregnancy-related complications (pre-eclampsia, intrauterine growth restriction). Interestingly, preliminary clinical data have associated certain 11beta-HSD1 gene polymorphisms with hypertensive disorders in pregnancy, suggesting, if confirmed by further targeted studies, it’s potential as a putative prognostic marker. Animal studies and observations in humans have confirmed that 11beta-HSD2 insufficiency is related with pregnancy adversity (pre-eclampsia, intrauterine growth restriction, preterm birth). Importantly, down-regulation or deficiency of placental 11beta-HSD2 is associated with significant restriction in fetal growth and low-birth weight, and unfavorable cardio-metabolic profile in adulthood. The potential association of 11beta-HSD1 tissue-specific dysregulation with gestational diabetes, as well as the plausible utility of 11beta-HSD2, as a biomarker of pregnancy adversity and later life morbidity, are emerging areas of intense scientific interest and future investigation. © 2016 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.
