TI  - Gender disparity in cardiovascular disease: Contributing facts and fables
PY  - 2016
T2  - Coronary Artery Disease: Characteristics, Management and Long-Term Outcomes
SP  - 83
EP  - 116
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85152556617&partnerID=40&md5=070911ac82a4125c0567b50fa148c6eb
AB  - Cardiovascular disease (CVD) affects men and women equally. Yet CVD is considered as a "Man's Disease". The patho-physiology of CVD differs significantly between the sexes. Thus American College of Cardiology (2011) laid specific guidelines focusing on women specific CVD prevention, diagnosis, management, treatment and outcomes. Compared to men, women are under-prevented, under-diagnosed, undertreated and under-studied with respect to CVD. Although women share similar risk factors like age, hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidemia, obesity, family history of CVD with men; women specific variables like depression, pre-eclampsia, gestational diabetes, thyroid disease, psoriasis, gout and auto immune disorders contribute significantly. Besides, inflammatory markers suggesting risk of disease like high sensitivity C reactive protein are higher in women at baseline then men. Estrogen and progesterone are more influential in women; menstruation can affect hematologic and electrocardiographic indices; women have reduced sympathetic and enhanced parasympathetic activity. In response to stress, women experience an increased pulse rate, resulting in an increased cardiac output; men result in increased vascular resistance resulting in increased blood pressure. Difference in pain perception exists between sexes, resulting in variability in presenting symptoms. Women are more likely to attribute their symptoms to non cardiac causes complicating timely treatment. Recent acute coronary syndrome registries have shown that on admission women are less likely to receive heparin, ACE inhibitors, glycoprotein IIa/IIIb inhibitors; at discharge less likely to be prescribed aspirin and statin therapy. Women experience more drug related adverse events than men. Many specific areas of CVD like heart failure, atrial fibrillation, valvular and structural disease, peripheral vascular and cerebro-vascular disease should be examined for existing gender differences. The healthcare sector holds a very critical and contributing role in education of adult female subjects towards risks of CVD; women specific contributing factors; warning signs; strategies to avoid delay in treatment; promote adherence to lifestyle interventions; ensure proper dosage adjustments to reduce risk of bleeding and other adverse events; promote primary and secondary prevention guidelines; improve application of evidence based therapy to improve women care in CVD. © 2016 Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved.
