AU  - Melnik, B.C.
TI  - The potential impact of maternal milk consumption during pregnancy on mTORC1-driven fetal growth
PY  - 2016
T2  - Human Fetal Growth and Development: First and Second Trimesters
SP  - 237
EP  - 258
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-319-14874-8_18
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85013054769&doi=10.1007%2f978-3-319-14874-8_18&partnerID=40&md5=d367eed4cb5aa98cbc8d0ed82128a569
AB  - The placenta regulates nutrient transfer to the fetus for fetal growth. Cell growth is orchestrated by the nutrient-sensitive kinase mechanistic target of rapamycin complex 1 (mTORC1). mTORC1 is activated by growth factors such as insulin and insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1), amino acids, predominantly leucine and glutamine, glucose, and saturated fatty acids, especially palmitate. mTORC1 is involved in the regulation of placenta growth as well as placental nutrient transfer important for mTORC1-driven fetal growth. Milk consumption activates mTORC1 of the milk recipient promoting growth and anabolism. In terms of evolutionary biology, persistent milk consumption of Homo sapiens is a recent behavioral change most recently boosted by the introduction of widespread refrigeration technology allowing permanent access to milk and dairy products during pregnancy. In comparison to all other mammals, humans are exceptional as they expose their pregnancy to the mTORC1-activating signaling system of bovine lactation. This chapter provides translational and epidemiological evidence that milk consumption during pregnancy increases maternal weight gain, promotes placental growth, fetal growth and birth weight as a result of exaggerated placental mTORC1-mediated nutrient transfer to the fetus. Increased birth weight and fetal macrosomia have been associated with the development of obesity, diabetes, allergy, arterial hypertension, cardiovascular diseases, and cancer in adult life. Milk consumption during pregnancy appears to upregulate mTORC1/FGF21-mediated glucose and mTORC1-driven amino acid transfer to the fetus. Milk consumption during pregnancy is a substantial health risk for the developing human fetus deviating appropriate metabolic programming that finally promotes mTORC1-driven diseases of civilization. © Springer International Publishing Switzerland 2016.
