UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85044258156&doi=10.1016%2fj.jogn.2016.02.004&partnerID=40&md5=66c9fefd103f026f4d688905f9b1f528
AB  - Pregnancy serves as a cardiometabolic stressor that may unmask underlying metabolic and vascular abnormalities in an evolving continuum of pathophysiology. In 2011, the American Heart Association indicated that a diagnosis of pre-eclampsia, gestational hypertension, or gestational diabetes classified a woman as at risk for cardiovascular disease. In this article, we discuss hypertensive disorders of pregnancy, gestational diabetes, and preterm birth as risk factors for future cardiovascular disease in women. © 2016 AWHONN, the Association of Women's Health, Obstetric and Neonatal Nurses
