TI  - Effect of Fiber and Low Glycemic Load Diet on Blood Glucose Profile and Cardiovascular Risk Factors in Diabetes and Poorly Controlled Diabetic Subjects
PY  - 2015
T2  - Glucose Intake and Utilization in Pre-Diabetes and Diabetes: Implications for Cardiovascular Disease
SP  - 133
EP  - 145
DO  - 10.1016/B978-0-12-800093-9.00011-9
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84942869608&doi=10.1016%2fB978-0-12-800093-9.00011-9&partnerID=40&md5=50f2d3368662724551b6e96d91786070
AB  - Diabetes, especially poorly controlled (HbA1c>8%), is a metabolic disease associated with a variety of micro- and macrovascular complications that is increasing worldwide. The conventional high carbohydrate intake recommended in diabetes results in suboptimal glycemic control and lipoprotein profile, gradually increasing the insulin and/or oral hypoglycemic medication requirement and eventually weight gain. Low glycemic load (GL) and high-fiber diets are alternative approaches for medical nutrition therapy. In prospective cohort studies, the high insoluble cereal dietary fiber and low GL diet, independent of known coronary disease risk factors, were associated with reduced risk of diabetes and coronary heart disease (CHD). However, there are meta-analyses and review studies reporting that "any beneficial effect of low glycemic index (GI)/GL diets on CHD and its risk factors is small." The inconsistent results of studies may be due to significantly varied criteria for low GI/GL and high GI/GL values in the diet among the studies. Cereal fiber and the low GL diet also are effective in blood glucose control of gestational diabetes mellitus patients who are unwilling to take blood glucose lowering medication. The mechanisms underlying improvement of blood glucose profile when consuming a low GL diet are probably due to reduced appetite, weight loss, and elevated whole-body glucose disposal through effects on circulating triacylglycerol and plasminogen activator inhibitor-1 (PAI-1, a marker of fibrinolytic capacity) concentration and suppression of hepatic triacylglycerol production. Higher concentrations of triacylglycerol and PAI-1 have a direct association with cardiovascular events. The mechanism behind the reduced diabetes risk of insoluble cereal dietary fiber is probably due to increased insulin sensitivity through changing gut microbial communities; it also involves hormonal and molecular factors. © 2015 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
