UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84902369997&doi=10.1007%2fs00129-013-3247-2&partnerID=40&md5=84f3b511d7b346821d5b3f29319b2f64
AB  - Egg donation (ED) offers new opportunities within reproductive medicine and yields high pregnancy rates per cycle. However, the resulting pregnancies are subject to a high rate of complications, the most frequent among them being preeclampsia, pregnancy-induced hypertension and gestational diabetes. Patients at risk for these conditions are women aged over 50 years, relevant concomitant diseases and multiple pregnancies. Women with 45X0 Turner syndrome or those with occult cardiac diseases bear an extremely-high risk, including pregnancy associated mortality. Perinatal results comprise elevated frequency of premature births and intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR). Possible consequences of these results include a limitation of recipients to patients under the age of 50 years, to promote single embryo transfer and to proceed with extreme caution and restraint in women with monosomy X. © Springer-Verlag 2014.
