AB  - Objective: To evaluate the impact of impaired GTT on pregnancy outcome, Setting : Al-Jalaa Maternity Hospital, Patients, material & methods: prospective study of 114 pregnant women diagnosed to have GIGTT in the period from Jan.2001 to Dec.2004 , the diagnosis of impaired GTT was performed according to new criteria of Multicentre European Study of European Association for Study of Diabetes ( EASD ) , which recommended higher cut-off values based primarily on 95th percentile after a 75 g OGTT rather than WHO criteria (1985) which were based on non pregnant values ). Controls were obtained by comparing with the next woman to deliver without IGTT. Results : Increased incidence of some pregnancy complications in association with GIGTT e.g. preeclampsia and premature labour ( #3fold ) , caesarean delivery (# 4 fold ) , prelabour rupture of membranes (PLROM ) and bad obstetric history (# 6 fold ) . Increased some neonatal complications with GIGTT e.g. macrosomia and congenital heart disease. Conclusion: IGTT classify the pregnancy as a high risk pregnancy which necessitates special antenatal care for less complications and better outcome.
