TI  - Fetal out come in caesarean section after abnormal cardiotocography
PY  - 2010
T2  - Medical Forum Monthly
VL  - 21
IS  - 8
SP  - 26
EP  - 30
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-78349234593&partnerID=40&md5=7497823c50ee01c645aa11c5d11bbac4
AB  - Background: Cardio-tocography (some times known as electronic fetal heart rate monitoring) record changes in fetal heart rate and their temporal relationship to uterine contraction. Cardio-tocography is the most commonly used test for ante-partum and intra-partum fetal surveillance because it gives information about fetal cerebral activity via cerebro-cardiac response, which is modified by hypoxia. Objective: To determine the correlation between abnormal CTG pattern and fetal outcome in patients delivered by caesarean section. Study design: Descriptive study. Place and duration of study: This study was carried out at department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Postgraduate Medical Institute, Lahore General Hospital, Lahore from April 1st, 2007 to March 31 st, 2008. Methodology: Two hundred consecutive patients with abnormal CTG delivered by Caesarean section were selected for study. Both labor and non-labor patients were included. Interpretation of CTG was done based on FIGO recommendation (1987) and was labeled as normal, suspicious and abnormal trace. Pregnancy outcome and neonatal data were obtained from delivery notes and neonatal charts and findings were correlated with fetal heart rate trace. Results: Out of 100 abnormal CTG, 68% had tachycardia, 76% had absent or reduced variability, 58% had decelerations and 20% had fetal bradycardia. The abnormal out come was highest in heart rate deceleration group. 10% babies were shifted to NICU. 9% developed neonatal complications including meconium aspiration syndrome 3%, early onset sepsis4%, and neonatal encephalopathy 2% while 91% babies had no neonatal complications. Abbreviations: CTG: Cardiotocography, FIGO: International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics, FHR: Fetal heart rate, NICU: Neonatal intensive care unit, GDM: Gestational Diabetes Mellitus, PROM: Pre-labor rupture of membranes, IUGR: Intra uterine growth retardation, SD: Standard deviation, CNS: Central nervous system. Conclusion: CTG can be continued as good screening test of fetal surveillance but it is not the sole criteria to influence the management of high risk pregnancies. Abnormal CTG should be supplemented with other test before intervention.
