AB  - The overview describes current advances in the study of pathogenesis of thromboembolic events in obstetrics. It outlines the specific features of hemocoagulation during normal pregnancy and postpartum. It also gives data on the change in hemocoagulation parameters in various pregnancy complications: habitual miscarriage, gestosis, gestational diabetes mellitus, development of fetoplacental insufficiency, fetal growth retardation syndrome, intrauterine fetal death, etc. Great emphasis is laid on thrombophilia as a condition induced by genetic defects in which the frequency of poor pregnancy outcomes increases. Risk factors for venous thromboembolism and arterial thrombosis in pregnant women and puerperas are shown. It is stressed that anticoagulant therapy (unfractionated heparin, low-molecular-weight heparins, warfarin, etc.) in obstetric complications requires up-to-date instrumental and laboratory control and is to be performed by highly skilled physicians. © 2010, Media Sphera Publishing Group. All rights reserved.
