AB  - Pregnancy frequently results in a decrease in sports activity. During this period, major physiological changes occur, which may influence the performance. However, maximum aerobic capacity does not change during pregnancy when compared to the postpartum period. The advantages of sports activity are common health benefits. Furthermore, regular exercise may play a role in the prevention of gestational diabetes and pre-eclampsia. The maternal disadvantages of sports activity are often musculoskeletal complaints and the risk of hypotension during exercise in supine position. Fetal complications are direct trauma, alteration of fetal heart rate, preterm delivery, intra-uterine growth restriction and teratogenic effects. In this article the type, duration and frequency of sports activities are discussed. The contraindications and warning signs to exercise during pregnancy are reviewed. Pregnant women should be encouraged to continue and engage in sports activity when the pregnancy is uncomplicated.
