TI  - Diabetes mellitus in Sub-Saharan Africa: A systematic review of the literature
ST  - Le diabète sucré en Afrique Sub-Saharienne, une revue systématique de la littérature
PY  - 2008
T2  - Louvain Medical
VL  - 127
IS  - 5
SP  - 153
EP  - 165
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-47149114345&partnerID=40&md5=f089fe29e2ac99a055fe497031fd0169
AB  - Objective: To examine the prevalence of diabetes mellitus and of intermediate dysglycemia in Sub-Saharan Africa. Materiels and methods: Prevalence studies that used WHO (World Health Organisation) diagnostic criteria and whose complete texts were available were evaluated. They were compiled through Medline search, lists of references of the authors, WHO and American Diabetes Association websites, and the 2000, 2003 and 2006 International Diabetes Federation (IDF) Diabetes Atlas. Six out of 23 studies had an acceptable non-response rate and a large sample size. Methods were heterogeneous, even when based on WHO criteria. In all but one study, only a single diagnostic procedure was used. Hence cases were probably underestimated. Only one study used both fasting glycemia and post-glucose challenge glycemia. Results: 23 studies were selected according to the search criteria. None was found in Central Africa. The prevalence of diabetes mellitus varies from 0.2 to 11.0 % in Southern Africa, from 3.4 to 8.3 % in Eastern Africa, and from 0.0 to 6.8 % in Western Africa. In Indians (Southern region) it ranges from 6.0 to 11.0 %, in descendants of Arabic origin (Eastern region) it is 8.3 %, and in Bantu it ranges from 2.4 to 4.2 % in the South and from 0.0 to 7.7 % in the West. Prevalence range from 2.7 to 11.0 % in an urban setting, and from 0.0 to 6.1 % in a rural environment. Diabetes is more prevalent in females in the West. No gender differences were observed else where. A high proportion of diabetes had not been previously detected. Impaired glucose tolerance ranges from 17.0 to 24.2 % in Indians from Southern Africa, and from 6.9 to 77 % in Bantu (1.8 to 10.6 % in the West; 7.6 to 8.0 % in the East, and 5.9 to 24.6 % in an urban environment). Impaired fasting glycemia ranges from 2.8 to 13.4 %; A single study that combined fasting and post-challenge glycemias has observed a 3.8 % prevalence of intermediate dysglycemia. Combined prevalences of diabetes and impaired glucose tolerance range from 8.6 % in Bantu, and 13.7 % in people of arabic origin, to 33.4 % in Indians. Gestational diabetes ranges from 1.7 to 3.7 %. Conclusion: Many studies are biased due to high non-response rates, and underestimate prevalence rates. This survey has observed prevalence rates that are often higher than those reported by IDF. Given the elevated prevalences of intermediate dysglycemia, the impact of diabetes and of cardiovascular diseases is bound to increase in Sub-Saharan Africa.
