AB  - Obese women with PCOS have a higher prevalence of menstrual disorders and infertility, are more likely to be hirsute and have lower SHBG levels, leading to higher serum concentrations of free testosterone, than lean women with PCOS. PCOS patients are predisposed to obesity. Obesity is an important independent risk factor for cardiovascular disease and type 2 diabetes. Women with PCOS have increased levels of cardiovascular risk factors: insulin resistance, obesity, dyslipidemia, hypertension and markers of abnormal vascular function. The presence of obesity in PCOS women has not only long-term metabolic health consequences but also important reproductive consequences including anovulation, infertility and miscarriage. Obese women with PCOS are less likely to respond to induction of ovulation treatment, require larger doses of gonadotropins to achieve ovulation and pregnancy and are more likely to miscarry. Furthermore, obesity in the overall population is associated with a higher prevalence of fetal abnormalities and more pregnancy complications including a higher risk for gestational diabetes. Weight loss as first therapeutic line may, at least partially, reverse the biochemical abnormalities (including insulin resistance, hyperlipidemia and dyslipidemia) and improve reproductive function in obese women with PCOS.
