AB  - Background. Adiponectin - contrary to a number of other cytokines/hormones secreted by fat tissue cells - has a beneficial effect in the pathogenesis of insulin resistance, type 2 diabetes mellitus, cardiovascular diseases through its influence on insulin sensitivity and protective role in the development of atherosclerosis. Material and methods. The aim of our study was the estimation of adiponectin concentrations in the peripheral blood of gestational diabetes patients in comparison to healthy pregnant women and the analysis of this cytokine alterations in relationship to particular phenotypic features (BMI, WHR), metabolic status, lipid parameters and indices of insulin resistance in pregnant women. Results. In the present study we have shown that adiponectin concentrations in the peripheral blood of women with gestational diabetes are significantly lower than in women without metabolic disturbances during pregnancy (11.3 ± ± 5.9 vs. 16.3 ± 6.0, p < 0.0001). Combined analysis of both studied groups revealed that there are statistically signifficant negative correlations between adiponectin and body mass index, waist circumference, waist-to-hip ratio or triglyceride levels. We also observed a positive correlation of the studied cytokine with HDL fraction of cholesterol. Conclusions. The results of our study confirm the recent publications about a key role of adiponectin in the pathogenesis of gestational diabetes and increased predisposition to type 2 diabetes mellitus and/or cardiovascular disease development in this group of women. Copyright © 2005 Via Medica.
