TI  - Pregnancy complications as a risk factor for metabolic and cardiovascular disease in later life
ST  - Zwangerschapscomplicaties als risicofactor voor metabole en cardiovasculaire aandoeningen op latere leeftijd
PY  - 2006
T2  - Nederlands Tijdschrift voor Geneeskunde
VL  - 150
IS  - 16
SP  - 898
EP  - 902
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-33646033449&partnerID=40&md5=4174d7b1d561c8de5348702c918ec885
AB  - In recent years several large epidemiological studies have been published that demonstrate that women who experience gestational diabetes, pregnancy-induced hypertension or pre-eclampsia have an increased risk of developing type-2 diabetes mellitus and cardiovascular disease. 15-50% of women who experience gestational diabetes develop type-2 diabetes mellitus; the risk is particularly high in those who require insulin therapy during pregnancy. Chronic hypertension frequently develops years after a pregnancy complicated by pregnancy-induced hypertension, especially in women who have had pregnancy-induced hypertension in multiple pregnancies. Women who experience pre-eclampsia in the first 36 weeks of pregnancy or in multiple pregnancies have an increased risk of cardiovascular morbidity and mortality in later life. Therefore gestational diabetes, pregnancy-induced hypertension and pre-eclampsia provide an opportunity to identify individuals with an increased risk of type-2 diabetes mellitus and cardiovascular disease at an early age. This may create new perspectives on prevention.
