TI  - Pheochromocytoma in a pregnant woman with a history of intracerebral aneurysms
PY  - 2005
T2  - American Journal of the Medical Sciences
VL  - 329
IS  - 6
SP  - 317
EP  - 319
DO  - 10.1097/00000441-200506000-00012
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-20544460422&doi=10.1097%2f00000441-200506000-00012&partnerID=40&md5=9ee6adaaea7e0567dbf01ddbc50b566a
AB  - Pheochromocytoma in pregnancy is rare; if unrecognized, potentially fatal hypertensive crises may occur. We report a case of a 35-year-old woman with a history of two intracerebral aneurysms who presented at 26 weeks' gestation with tachycardia, hypertension, and pulmonary edema. Laboratory data revealed elevated 24-hour urinary catecholamine and metanephrine levels, and abdominal sonography showed a 10-cm right adrenal mass. After stabilization with phenoxybenzamine and metoprolol, cesarean section was successfully performed at 36 weeks' gestation. Postpartum abdominal computed tomography scanning confirmed a 10-cm right adrenal mass. A benign pheochromocytoma was removed without incident. This case illustrates the importance of early diagnosis and management of pheochromocytoma in pregnancy and is also an example of the rare association of pheochromocytoma with intracerebral aneurysms.; AuthorsConclusions:In summary, we report a case of a pregnant woman with a large pheochromocytoma and a prior history of two cerebral aneurysms who presented in middle-to-late gestation and was successfully managed through pregnancy, cesarean section, and postpartum adrenalectomy. The case illustrates the importance of recognizing and treating pheochromocytoma in pregnancy to avoid maternal and fetal morbidity and mortality.; FreeText:Patient presented at 26 weeks' gestation with acute pulmonary edema, episodic tachycardia, and severe hypertension. There was initial improvement after treatment with oxygen and furosemide, but symptoms as well as tachycardia and hypertension recurred. Treatment with labetalol, hydralazine, and methyldopa was then initiated, and magnesium sulfate was given for seizure prophylaxis. At age 28, she was found to have left internal carotid and right posterior communicating artery aneurysms on a magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scan of the head. Her headaches persisted after aneurysm surgery and progressively worsened and increased in frequency over the last few years, particularly during the second trimester of the current pregnancy. An abdominal sonogram demonstrated a 10-cm, solid, echogenic mass with increased vascularity in the region of the right adrenal gland. MRI scan of the abdomen was not performed because of the presence of metallic cerebral aneurysm clips. Labetalol and methyldopa were then discontinued because these drugs may interfere in several of the assays for catecholamines and their metabolites. Hydralazine was discontinued and phenoxybenzamine 10 mg twice daily (BID) and Lopresor 25 mg BID were started on the fifth hospital day. She was found to have gestational diabetes on an oral glucose tolerance test, and this was adequately managed with diet.; Indications:One pregnant patient with hypertension with coexisting acute pulmonary edema, episodic tachycardia, and pheochromocytoma.; Patients:One 35-year-old woman (inpatient and outpatient).; TypeofStudy:A case report describing a pregnant woman with a history of intracerebral aneurysms who had pheochromocytoma presenting with hypertension which was treated by Lopresor.; DosageDuration:25 mg bid (=50 mg daily) for 3 days then increased to 50 mg bid (=100 mg daily). Duration: about 10 weeks.; Results:Normalization of blood pressure was noted by the 7th hospital day, with a pulse rate around 100/min; Lopresor was increased to 50 mg BID on the 8th hospital day, with a pulse rate of approximately 80 to 90/min. On this regimen, she was mildly orthostatic. Because of marked improvement with medications, it was decided to continue alpha- and beta-adrenergic blockade and perform adrenalectomy in the postpartum period. She was discharged home at 28 weeks of gestation and continued to be asymptomatic. Cesarean section was elected to avoid precipitation of a hypertensive crisis by prolonged labor and vaginal delivery, and because of her history of intracranial aneurysms. After establishment of fetal lung maturity by anmiocentesis, cesarean section was performed at 36 weeks' gestation under epidural anesthesia using mepivicaine and fentanyl. A healthy female neonate weighing 3135 g was delivered; Apgar scores were 9 at both 1 minute and 5 minutes. The intraoperative, immediate postoperative, and postpartum courses were uneventful. A computed tomography scan of the abdomen obtained in the postpartum period showed a right adrenal mass displacing the right kidney inferiorly and containing central areas of necrosis. Open right adrenalectomy was performed at 8 weeks postpartum without incident. Pathologic examination showed a 12-cm pheochromocytoma with no evidence of vascular invasion and with surgical margins negative for malignancy. Her diabetes has also resolved. She remains asymptomatic with no evidence of tumor recurrence, tachycardia, or hypertension.; AdverseEffects:1 patient had mildly orthostatic.
