TI  - Doppler studies in pregnancies complicated by diabetes mellitus
PY  - 1997
T2  - Prenatal and Neonatal Medicine
VL  - 2
IS  - 3
SP  - 200
EP  - 204
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-1842740069&partnerID=40&md5=54a1c392106985483696b2a2ed04286d
AB  - Objective: The object of this study was to determine the relationship between the placental resistance and the severity of diabetes mellitus according to White's classification and the values of hemoglobin (Hb)A1c. Study design During the third trimester of pregnancy, 631 Doppler umbilical artery blood flow studies were performed in 115 diabetic pregnant women (White class A1, 39; A2, 46; B, 12; C, 5; D, 9; F, 1; R, 3). Our patients were divided in three groups. Group 1 consisted of 85 patients with gestational diabetes controlled either with diet (class A1, n = 39) or insulin (class A2, n = 45). Group 2 consisted of 17 patients with pregestational diabetes without vasculopathy (class B, n = 12; class C, n = 5) and Group 3 consisted of 13 patients with pregestational diabetes with vasculopathy (class D, n = 9; class F, n = 1; class R, n = 3). Results: In group 1 the mean systolic/diastolic (S/D) ratio of the umbilical artery was 2.6 ± 0.4, in Group 2 3.7 ± 0.3 and in Group 3 3.2 ± 0.5. These values were statistically significant (p < 0.0001) with linear increase related to the severity of disease. The percentage of abnormal S/D ratios (> 3) was 7.1, 17.7 and 53.9%, respectively, in the three groups (linear trend x = 19, p < 0.00001). In Group 1, the mean value of HbA1c was 4.8 ± 0.8%, in Group 2 6.3 ± 1.7% and in Group 3 6.0 ± 1.3%. Subdivision of patients on the basis of the value of HbA1c (normal range 3.5-5.5% into well-controlled (n = 87 cases, HbA1c = 4.6 ± 0.6%) and poorly controlled groups (n = 23 cases, HbA1c = 6.9 ± 1%) revealed a statistically significant difference in S/D values (2.6 ± 0.4 vs. 2.9 ± 0.5, p < 0.002). A significant positive correlation was found between the S/D ratio and the value of HbA1c (r = 0.30, p < 0.001). In five cases, the data relating to the control of diabetes were incomplete because the patients were followed up elsewhere. Using multiple regression analysis, a significant positive correlation (p < 10) was found between S/D ratio and White's classification adjusted for maternal age and parity. Conclusions: From these data, we can conclude that: (1) Normal S/D ratios can be expected in gestational diabetes with good control; (2) There is a linear correlation between the S/D ratio and the severity of the disease as determined by White's classification; and (3) There is a linear correlation between poorly controlled diabetes mellitus (HbA1c > 5.5%) and increased placental resistance (S/D > 3) when the patients were subdivided on the basis of the HbA1c value.
