TI  - The postprandial state - A critical phase of the metabolic regulation in diabetes: Consequences for the vessel risk
ST  - Der postprandiale status - Eine kritische phase der stoffwechselregulation bei diabetes: Konsequenzen fur das gefassrisiko
PY  - 1999
T2  - Diabetes und Stoffwechsel
VL  - 8
IS  - SUPPL. 6
SP  - 3
EP  - 7
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-0032760456&partnerID=40&md5=2e7f42d615947d78dcbf0fe04481fc96
AB  - The postprandial state in diabetes plays a central role in the control of HbA(1c), blood lipids, coagulation and redox-balance. The changes in blood sugar control result in disturbed removal of triglyceride-rich lipoproteins, hypercoagulation, impaired fibrinolysis and increased production of free radicals. The early insulin secretion phase (0-30 minutes prandial) was shown to be of crucial importance for the quality of postprandial metabolism. The complex anomalies in the regulation of the postprandial state sustain the insulin resistance and in addition damage the beta-cells. In parallel the postprandial state is associated with endothelial functional impairment. Many epidemiologic studies have shown that postprandial glucose, triglycerides and coagulation parameters correlate more closely to impaired endothelial function and atherosclerotic vessel alterations than the fasting values of these parameters. The Diabetes Intervention Study was the first to demonstrate that postprandial blood glucose but not fasting glucose is an independent risk factor for cardiovascular and total mortality. The monitoring of postprandial blood glucose in gestational diabetes led to improved HbA(1c)-values, less anomalies of the babies and less birth complications than the monitoring of preprandial blood glucose. Therefore, postprandial glycemic control is essential for the HbA(1c)-values and for the prevention of late complications. This should be more strongly considered in the differential therapy with oral anditiabetic drugs and short acting insulins.
