TI  - Insulin resistance syndrome
PY  - 2001
T2  - American Family Physician
VL  - 63
IS  - 6
SP  - 1159
EP  - 1163
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-0035868740&partnerID=40&md5=170aec4d4e6055ecf5b9dc31ee5e8172
AB  - Insulin resistance can be linked to diabetes, hypertension, dyslipidemia, cardiovascular disease and other abnormalities. These abnormalities constitute the insulin resistance syndrome. Because resistance usually develops long before these diseases appear, identifying and treating insulin-resistant patients has potentially great preventive value. Insulin resistance should be suspected in patients with a history of diabetes in first-degree relatives; patients with a personal history of gestational diabetes, polycystic ovary syndrome or impaired glucose tolerance; and obese patients, particularly those with abdominal obesity. Present treatment consists of sensible lifestyle changes, including weight loss to attain healthy body weight, 30 minutes of accumulated moderate-intensity physical activity per day and increased dietary fiber intake. Pharmacotherapy is not currently recommended for patients with isolated insulin resistance.
