UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-0027740390&partnerID=40&md5=f1bd234b5176c7e15b54e8398efef7f7
AB  - The aim of this study was to assess the benefits and warnings of DIABCARE Data Set (DDS) collection using the DIABCARE program adapted to a net environment in a hospital based outpatient consultation. Experience with 293 patients is reported with 84.6% of the records completed. The first benefit is DDS exploitation as a registry. This showed that 4.4% of the patients skipped the visit. Diabetes-type characterisation was: 43% IDDM, 28.9% NIDDM (other types 27.6%). Up to 77.1% of IDDM patients received treatment with multiple injection therapy (≥3 injections/day), and 88.8% NIDDM received oral agents and/or insulin. The blood glucose self monitoring status was 98% in IDDM and 80.5% in NIDDM. Status of St. Vincent Declaration targets showed blindness 2.1%/7.9%, end-stage renal failure 2%/1.5%, amputation 0%/0%, myocardial infarction 1%/15.2%, stroke 0%/7.6% in IDDM and NIDDM patients, respectively, and outcome for pregnancies ending in the last year: normal in women with IDDM and 6.7% abortion rate in women with gestational diabetes mellitus. The warnings from the analysis of IDDM and NIDDM patients derive from incomplete files for some risk factors or process indicators: i.e. registry of cigarette smoking status was lacking in 19.4% of patients, proteinuria/albuminuria in 17.1%, fundoscopy in 13.8%. This analysis allowed quantification of risk groups: i.e. active smokers 39.8%; previous hypoglycaemic emergency 8.4%; patients with feet at risk 31.5% (either abnormal sensitivity, absent foot pulses or acute/healed ulcers); risk of blindness 12.6% (maculopathy, preproliferative retinopathy, proliferative retinopathy or advanced eye disease). On the basis of this information, intervention and initiation of quality assurance circles can be programmed.
