TI  - Risk factors for gestational diabetes in black population
PY  - 1990
T2  - Diabetes Care
VL  - 13
IS  - 11 SUPPL. 4
SP  - 1196
EP  - 1201
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-0025163055&partnerID=40&md5=0c19bcc86cb7addac60651f8655071e9
AB  - In a long-term longitudinal study of gestational diabetes mellitus in Black women, risk factors that were identified were age, obesity, a family history of diabetes, and the presence of hypertension. Poor predictors were a history of a previous large-for-date infant, parity, and age at first pregnancy. The prevalence of smooth muscle and nuclear autoantibodies was higher in gestational diabetic subjects. Gestational diabetic subjects who required insulin for glycemic control were more obese, had a lower frequency of the Bf-F phenotype and a higher frequency of the Bf-F1 phenotype, and had a lower frequency of the type 2 allele at the polymorphic locus adjacent to the insulin gene. Restriction-fragment-length polymorphisms flanking the insulin and apolipoprotein A-I and C-III genes, although not associated with gestational diabetes mellitus, may be associated with hyperlipidemia and subsequent atherosclerosis.
